---
import Architecture from '../../layouts/Architecture.astro';
import Diagram from '../../components/arch/Diagram.astro';
import Label from '../../components/arch/Label.astro';
import Callout from '../../components/arch/Callout.astro';

// Lane chart geometry. Time maps linearly onto x between X0 and X1; the
// landed stamp of each lane sits in its own column at STAMP_X.
const X0 = 80;
const X1 = 620;
const STAMP_X = 650;
const LANE_H = 36;
const LANE_GAP = 12;
const LANE_Y0 = 64;
const SPAN_H = 24;
const SPIN_OUT = 167; // estimate: 5 wraps at about half a cell per frame at 60 fps
const BOUNCE = 600;

type Lane = {
  i: number;
  delay: number;
  spinIn: number;
  spinDone: number;
  teaseStart?: number;
  teaseEnd?: number;
  spinOutAt: number;
  land: number;
  landed: number;
};

// NORMAL profile, 5 reels, setResult at t = 1000.
const normal: Lane[] = [0, 1, 2, 3, 4].map((i) => {
  const delay = i * 100;
  const spinIn = delay + 350;
  const spinDone = Math.max(1000, spinIn + 500);
  const spinOutAt = spinDone + i * 140;
  const land = spinOutAt + SPIN_OUT;
  return { i, delay, spinIn, spinDone, spinOutAt, land, landed: land + BOUNCE };
});

// Same spin with setAnticipation([3, 4]): legacy tease, 450 ms, 30% speed.
const teased: Lane[] = normal.map((l) => {
  if (l.i < 3) return l;
  const teaseStart = l.spinDone;
  const teaseEnd = teaseStart + 450;
  const spinOutAt = teaseEnd + l.i * 140;
  const land = spinOutAt + 555; // spin-out at speed 9, about 33 frames
  return { ...l, teaseStart, teaseEnd, spinOutAt, land, landed: land + BOUNCE };
});

const laneY = (i: number) => LANE_Y0 + i * (LANE_H + LANE_GAP);
const spanY = (i: number) => laneY(i) + (LANE_H - SPAN_H) / 2;
const scale = (T: number) => (t: number) => X0 + (t / T) * (X1 - X0);
const BOTTOM = laneY(4) + LANE_H;
---
<Architecture
  title="A spin in milliseconds"
  subtitle="One ordinary spin, replayed with a stopwatch, from the button press to spin:complete."
  eyebrow="Milliseconds"
>
  <p>
    The <a href="/architecture/spin-lifecycle/">lifecycle page</a> explains the
    rules. This page plays one spin back in slow motion and puts a timestamp on
    every moment the player notices: the reels lurching into motion one after
    another, the blur, the first reel dropping while the others are still
    flying, the last bounce settling. Then it replays the same spin twice more,
    once with two reels teasing and once with a skip press in the middle, and
    ends with what the faster profiles change.
  </p>

  <p>
    The setup is deliberately ordinary. Five reels on the <code>NORMAL</code>
    profile, the tween motion model at 60 fps (so one frame is 16.7 ms), three
    visible cells with one buffer cell above and below so every result frame is
    five symbols long, no reel groups, no stop-delay override, and the server
    answering at <code>t = 1000</code>, one second after the press. Every number
    below is arithmetic on the profile except one: the spin-out, the stretch
    where the reel scrolls the result symbols into view. Five symbol wraps at
    about half a cell per frame take about ten frames, roughly 167 ms, but the
    exact figure depends on where within a cell the reel happens to be when its
    frame is queued. Those numbers are marked "about".
  </p>

  <Callout kind="plain">
    Three clocks run during a spin and they do not start together. The start
    stagger is counted from the press. The minimum spin time is counted from
    the moment each reel is up to speed. The stop delay is counted from the
    moment each reel is allowed to stop. Most "why did that land then?"
    questions come down to which clock was still running.
  </Callout>

  <h2>The replay, lane by lane</h2>

  <p>
    Figure 1 is the whole spin on one chart, one lane per reel, time running
    left to right. Look at two things. The start spans step down the left like
    a staircase: that is the 100 ms stagger the player sees as "one after
    another". And the landings on the right are not evenly spaced: reels 0 and
    1 land 140 ms apart, but from reel 2 onward the gap grows to 240 ms. The
    section after the table explains why.
  </p>

  <Diagram height={390} caption="NORMAL, result at t = 1000. Start phases stagger by 100 ms, the stop gate opens at 1000, and the stop delays stack on top of each reel's own minimum spin time, so landings spread 240 ms apart from reel 2 on. The right column is each reel's spin:reelLanded stamp.">
    {(() => {
      const T = 2700;
      const X = scale(T);
      return (
        <>
          {/* time axis */}
          {[0, 500, 1000, 1500, 2000, 2500].map((t) => (
            <>
              <line x1={X(t)} y1={50} x2={X(t)} y2={BOTTOM + 6} class="guide" />
              <Label x={X(t)} y={40} text={`${t}`} anchor="middle" mono tone="faint" />
            </>
          ))}
          <Label x={STAMP_X} y={40} text="landed" mono tone="faint" />

          {/* lanes */}
          {normal.map((l) => (
            <>
              <rect x={X0} y={laneY(l.i)} width={X1 - X0} height={LANE_H} rx={2} class="lane-bg" />
              <Label x={20} y={laneY(l.i) + 23} text={`reel ${l.i}`} mono tone="fg" weight={600} />
              <rect x={X(l.delay)} y={spanY(l.i)} width={X(l.spinIn) - X(l.delay)} height={SPAN_H} rx={2} class="span-start" />
              <rect x={X(l.spinIn)} y={spanY(l.i)} width={X(l.spinDone) - X(l.spinIn)} height={SPAN_H} rx={2} class="span-spin" />
              {l.spinOutAt > l.spinDone && (
                <rect x={X(l.spinDone)} y={spanY(l.i)} width={X(l.spinOutAt) - X(l.spinDone)} height={SPAN_H} rx={2} class="span-rest" />
              )}
              <rect x={X(l.spinOutAt)} y={spanY(l.i)} width={X(l.land) - X(l.spinOutAt)} height={SPAN_H} rx={2} class="span-stop" />
              <rect x={X(l.land)} y={spanY(l.i)} width={X(l.landed) - X(l.land)} height={SPAN_H} rx={2} class="span-bounce" />
              {/* event ticks: spin:stopping, spin:reelLanding, spin:reelLanded */}
              <line x1={X(l.spinDone)} y1={laneY(l.i) - 3} x2={X(l.spinDone)} y2={laneY(l.i) + 4} class="guide-strong" />
              <line x1={X(l.land)} y1={laneY(l.i) - 3} x2={X(l.land)} y2={laneY(l.i) + 4} class="guide-strong" />
              <line x1={X(l.landed)} y1={laneY(l.i) - 3} x2={X(l.landed)} y2={laneY(l.i) + 4} class="guide-strong" />
              <Label x={STAMP_X} y={laneY(l.i) + 23} text={`${l.landed}`} mono tone="faint" />
            </>
          ))}

          {/* global events */}
          <line x1={X(750)} y1={50} x2={X(750)} y2={BOTTOM + 6} class="edge edge-accent edge-dashed" />
          <line x1={X(1000)} y1={50} x2={X(1000)} y2={BOTTOM + 6} class="edge edge-primary" />
          <line x1={X(2577)} y1={50} x2={X(2577)} y2={BOTTOM + 6} class="edge edge-primary edge-dashed" />
          <Label x={X(0)} y={BOTTOM + 26} text="spin:start 0" mono />
          <Label x={X(1000)} y={BOTTOM + 26} text="setResult 1000" anchor="middle" mono tone="fg" weight={600} />
          <Label x={X(2577)} y={BOTTOM + 26} text="spin:complete 2577" anchor="end" mono tone="fg" weight={600} />
          <Label x={X(750)} y={BOTTOM + 48} text="spin:allStarted 750" anchor="middle" mono />

          {/* key */}
          {([
            ['span-start', 'start (pull + accelerate)', 80],
            ['span-spin', 'spin', 300],
            ['span-rest', 'stop delay', 370],
            ['span-stop', 'spin-out', 480],
            ['span-bounce', 'bounce', 580],
          ] as const).map(([cls, label, x]) => (
            <>
              <rect x={x} y={BOTTOM + 68} width={14} height={13} rx={2} class={cls} />
              <Label x={x + 20} y={BOTTOM + 79} text={label} />
            </>
          ))}
        </>
      );
    })()}
  </Diagram>

  <p>
    Now the same spin as a commentary. Every reel's chain is created in the
    same tick; the staircase comes from each start phase's delay step, which
    on NORMAL is <code>i * 100</code> ms. The right-hand column lists the
    events your game would hear at each moment.
  </p>
  <div class="not-prose my-6">
    <div class="rounded-lg border border-border bg-card overflow-hidden">
      <table class="w-full text-sm">
        <thead class="bg-muted/60">
          <tr>
            <th class="text-left px-4 py-2 font-mono text-[11px] uppercase tracking-wider text-muted-foreground">t (ms)</th>
            <th class="text-left px-4 py-2 font-mono text-[11px] uppercase tracking-wider text-muted-foreground">What happens</th>
            <th class="text-left px-4 py-2 font-mono text-[11px] uppercase tracking-wider text-muted-foreground">Events</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {[
            ['0', 'The press. spin() captures the active profile and creates five chains. Reel 0 has no delay, so it goes at once: launch, then the pull (the small backward tug, speed to -2 over 50 ms, power1.out), then accelerate (up to 30 over 300 ms, power2.in), then announce.', 'spin:start; reel 0 phase:enter start'],
            ['100, 200, 300, 400', 'The staircase. Reels 1 to 4 finish their delay step one by one and run the same launch, pull, accelerate, announce sequence. To the player, each column tugs back and flies a tenth of a second after its neighbour.', 'per-reel phase:step delay end, launch, pull...'],
            ['350', 'Reel 0 is at full speed. It leaves start and enters spin, and its 500 ms minimum starts counting now, in ticker deltas.', 'reel 0 phase:exit start, phase:enter spin; spinStart on the reel'],
            ['450, 550, 650', 'Reels 1, 2, 3 reach full speed and enter spin, each starting its own 500 ms clock.', 'phase:enter spin'],
            ['750', 'Reel 4, the last one, enters spin. All five are blurring. The controller announces it and tries the stop gate, but there is no result yet, so the gate stays closed.', 'spin:allStarted'],
            ['1000', 'The server answers: setResult(). Big symbols are coordinated, one frame is built and cached per reel, and every spin phase is told it may finish. Reel 0 has spun 650 ms and reel 1 550 ms, both past their 500 ms minimum, so both complete now. Reel 2 has only 450 on its clock, reel 3 350, reel 4 250; they keep blurring.', 'reels 0 and 1: phase:exit spin, spin:stopping'],
            ['1000', 'Reel 0 enters stop with a delay of 0, so its spin-out starts at once: the result symbols begin scrolling in. Reel 1 enters stop with a 140 ms delay and keeps spinning at full speed for now.', 'phase:enter stop'],
            ['1050, 1150, 1250', 'Reels 2, 3 and 4 reach their minimum one after another and complete spin. Their stop delays (280, 420, 560) start counting now, not at 1000.', 'spin:stopping(2), (3), (4)'],
            ['about 1167', 'The first landing. Reel 0 has scrolled its last result symbol into place: it halts, snaps to the grid, and the bounce begins (22 px out and back over 220 ms, power1.out). The player sees the first column settle while four are still flying.', 'spin:reelLanding(0)'],
            ['1140, 1330, 1570, 1810', 'Reels 1 to 4 finish their stop delays and begin spinning out.', 'phase:step spinOut start'],
            ['about 1307, 1497, 1737, 1977', 'Reels 1 to 4 land and start bouncing, left to right.', 'spin:reelLanding(1..4)'],
            ['about 1387', 'Reel 0 finishes its bounce. Its stop phase completes and the controller marks it landed.', 'phase:exit stop, spin:reelLanded(0)'],
            ['about 1527, 1717, 1957', 'Reels 1, 2 and 3 finish their bounces.', 'spin:reelLanded(1..3)'],
            ['about 2197', 'Reel 4 settles. Marking it landed, the controller sees every reel down and finishes the spin. Your await returns.', 'spin:reelLanded(4), spin:allLanded, spin:complete; the promise resolves'],
          ].map(([t, what, ev]) => (
            <tr class="border-t border-border/60">
              <td class="px-4 py-2.5 font-mono text-primary text-xs whitespace-nowrap">{t}</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-xs text-foreground/80">{what}</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-2.5 font-mono text-xs text-muted-foreground">{ev}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </div>
  <p>
    The promise resolves with
    <code>{'{ wasSkipped: false, skipMode: null, skipContext: null, duration: about 2577 }'}</code>.
    The second table is the same replay reduced to one row per reel, which is
    the handy form when you are checking your own event log against it.
  </p>
  <div class="not-prose my-6">
    <div class="rounded-lg border border-border bg-card overflow-hidden">
      <table class="w-full text-sm">
        <thead class="bg-muted/60">
          <tr>
            {['Reel', 'spin:stopping', 'Stop delay', 'Spin-out starts', 'Land (spin:reelLanding)', 'Bounce done (spin:reelLanded)'].map((h) => (
              <th class="text-left px-4 py-2 font-mono text-[11px] uppercase tracking-wider text-muted-foreground">{h}</th>
            ))}
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {normal.map((l) => (
            <tr class="border-t border-border/60">
              <td class="px-4 py-2.5 font-mono text-primary text-xs">{l.i}</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-2.5 font-mono text-xs text-foreground/80">{l.spinDone}</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-2.5 font-mono text-xs text-foreground/80">{l.i * 140}</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-2.5 font-mono text-xs text-foreground/80">{l.spinOutAt}</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-2.5 font-mono text-xs text-foreground/80">about {l.land}</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-2.5 font-mono text-xs text-foreground/80">about {l.landed}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>Why 240 ms apart, not 140</h2>

  <p>
    The profile says <code>stopDelay: 140</code>, so you might expect the five
    landings to be 140 ms apart. Reels 0 and 1 are. The rest are 240 ms apart,
    and the reason is that two staggers add up. The stop delay is a step inside
    the stop phase, and the stop phase cannot begin until the spin phase has
    completed. When the result arrives early, reels 2 to 4 are still inside
    their minimum spin time, and that minimum started at a staggered moment
    (reel 2 entered spin at 550, reel 3 at 650, reel 4 at 750). So reel 3's
    clock reads: minimum reached at 1150, plus its 420 ms stop delay, spin-out
    at 1570. Reel 2's reads 1050 + 280 = 1330. That is 100 ms from the start
    side plus 140 ms from the stop side, 240 ms between neighbours. Reels 0
    and 1 had both already passed 500 ms when the result came, so only the stop
    stagger separates them.
  </p>

  <p>
    A late result removes the first term. With <code>setResult()</code> at
    <code>t = 3000</code>, every reel's minimum is long past, so every spin
    phase completes at 3000 and the five stop delays all start from the same
    instant. Reel 4 begins its spin-out at 3560, lands at about 3727 and
    finishes bouncing at about 4327, which is when <code>spin:complete</code>
    fires. Landings are then exactly 140 ms apart.
  </p>

  <Callout kind="why">
    The minimum spin time is counted per reel, from the moment that reel is up
    to speed, not from the press. That is what guarantees every column blurs
    for at least half a second no matter how fast the server is. The side
    effect is the extra 100 ms per landing when the result is early. If you
    want landings exactly <code>stopDelay</code> apart regardless of latency,
    hold your <code>setResult()</code> call until every reel has been spinning
    for the minimum: on NORMAL, <code>spin:allStarted</code> plus 500 ms.
  </Callout>

  <h2>Replay two: reels 3 and 4 tease</h2>

  <p>
    Same spin, but this time the game calls <code>setAnticipation([3, 4])</code>
    after <code>spin()</code> and before the result, with the default stagger
    of 0 and the default tease shape. What the player sees: reels 0 to 2 land
    exactly as before, then reels 3 and 4 slow to a crawl instead of dropping,
    hold there, and creep onto their symbols. Figure 2 adds that tease span to
    the chart. Notice how much longer the right-hand lanes get: the spin now
    ends about 840 ms later.
  </p>

  <Diagram height={390} caption="setAnticipation([3, 4]) on NORMAL. Each tease is 450 ms at 30% speed, then the stop delay runs at that tease speed, and the spin-out crawls onto the frame at speed 9 with preserveSpeed. anticipation:reel fires at 1150 for reel 3 and 1250 for reel 4; the spin ends about 840 ms later than without the tease.">
    {(() => {
      const T = 3500;
      const X = scale(T);
      return (
        <>
          {[0, 500, 1000, 1500, 2000, 2500, 3000, 3500].map((t) => (
            <>
              <line x1={X(t)} y1={50} x2={X(t)} y2={BOTTOM + 6} class="guide" />
              <Label x={X(t)} y={40} text={`${t}`} anchor="middle" mono tone="faint" />
            </>
          ))}
          <Label x={STAMP_X} y={40} text="landed" mono tone="faint" />
          {teased.map((l) => (
            <>
              <rect x={X0} y={laneY(l.i)} width={X1 - X0} height={LANE_H} rx={2} class="lane-bg" />
              <Label x={20} y={laneY(l.i) + 23} text={`reel ${l.i}`} mono tone="fg" weight={600} />
              <rect x={X(l.delay)} y={spanY(l.i)} width={X(l.spinIn) - X(l.delay)} height={SPAN_H} rx={2} class="span-start" />
              <rect x={X(l.spinIn)} y={spanY(l.i)} width={X(l.spinDone) - X(l.spinIn)} height={SPAN_H} rx={2} class="span-spin" />
              {l.teaseStart !== undefined && l.teaseEnd !== undefined && (
                <rect x={X(l.teaseStart)} y={spanY(l.i)} width={X(l.teaseEnd) - X(l.teaseStart)} height={SPAN_H} rx={2} class="span-anticipation" />
              )}
              {(() => {
                const from = l.teaseEnd ?? l.spinDone;
                return l.spinOutAt > from ? (
                  <rect x={X(from)} y={spanY(l.i)} width={X(l.spinOutAt) - X(from)} height={SPAN_H} rx={2} class="span-rest" />
                ) : null;
              })()}
              <rect x={X(l.spinOutAt)} y={spanY(l.i)} width={X(l.land) - X(l.spinOutAt)} height={SPAN_H} rx={2} class="span-stop" />
              <rect x={X(l.land)} y={spanY(l.i)} width={X(l.landed) - X(l.land)} height={SPAN_H} rx={2} class="span-bounce" />
              <line x1={X(l.spinDone)} y1={laneY(l.i) - 3} x2={X(l.spinDone)} y2={laneY(l.i) + 4} class="guide-strong" />
              <line x1={X(l.land)} y1={laneY(l.i) - 3} x2={X(l.land)} y2={laneY(l.i) + 4} class="guide-strong" />
              <line x1={X(l.landed)} y1={laneY(l.i) - 3} x2={X(l.landed)} y2={laneY(l.i) + 4} class="guide-strong" />
              <Label x={STAMP_X} y={laneY(l.i) + 23} text={`${l.landed}`} mono tone="faint" />
            </>
          ))}
          <line x1={X(1000)} y1={50} x2={X(1000)} y2={BOTTOM + 6} class="edge edge-primary" />
          <line x1={X(3415)} y1={50} x2={X(3415)} y2={BOTTOM + 6} class="edge edge-primary edge-dashed" />
          <Label x={X(1000)} y={BOTTOM + 26} text="setResult 1000" anchor="middle" mono tone="fg" weight={600} />
          <Label x={X(3415)} y={BOTTOM + 26} text="spin:complete 3415" anchor="end" mono tone="fg" weight={600} />
          {([
            ['span-start', 'start', 80],
            ['span-spin', 'spin', 155],
            ['span-anticipation', 'tease', 225],
            ['span-rest', 'stop delay', 300],
            ['span-stop', 'spin-out', 410],
            ['span-bounce', 'bounce', 506],
          ] as const).map(([cls, label, x]) => (
            <>
              <rect x={x} y={BOTTOM + 68} width={14} height={13} rx={2} class={cls} />
              <Label x={x + 20} y={BOTTOM + 79} text={label} />
            </>
          ))}
        </>
      );
    })()}
  </Diagram>

  <p>
    <strong>t = 1150, reel 3 starts to hesitate.</strong> Its spin phase
    completes. It is on the tease list and the hold (the profile's
    <code>anticipationDelay</code>, 450 ms) is positive, so it teases. It waits
    out its stagger offset (0, since it is first in tease order), joins the
    teasing set, tells its symbols the tease has begun, and emits
    <code>anticipation:reel {'{ reelIndex: 3, order: 0, total: 2 }'}</code>
    followed by <code>spin:stopping(3)</code>. The default tease shape then
    eases the speed from 30 down to 9 over the first 35% of the hold (157.5 ms,
    <code>power2.out</code>) and holds there for the remaining 65% (292.5 ms).
    The tease ends at 1600, and because it played to the end, reel 3 is marked
    as having anticipated.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>1600 to 2020, reel 3 creeps.</strong> The stop phase begins, and its
    420 ms delay step runs while the reel keeps crawling at speed 9. At 2020
    the spin-out starts with <code>preserveSpeed</code>, so instead of snapping
    back to full speed the reel keeps its crawl, clamped to
    <code>min(max(9, 30 * 0.08), 30) = 9</code>. At 0.15 of a cell per frame,
    five wraps take about 33 frames, roughly 555 ms: the player watches the
    result symbols inch into view. The reel lands at about 2575
    (<code>spin:reelLanding(3)</code>), bounces for 600 ms, and at about 3175
    <code>anticipation:reelEnd {'{ reelIndex: 3 }'}</code> fires, followed by
    <code>spin:reelLanded(3)</code>.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>t = 1250, reel 4 follows the same script</strong> one step behind:
    <code>anticipation:reel {'{ 4, 1, 2 }'}</code>, tease until 1700, a 560 ms
    stop delay until 2260, a crawling spin-out to about 2815, a bounce to about
    3415. Then <code>anticipation:reelEnd(4)</code>, <code>spin:reelLanded(4)</code>,
    <code>spin:allLanded</code> and <code>spin:complete</code>. Had the game
    asked for <code>stagger: 'sequential'</code> instead, reel 4's tease would
    not even begin until reel 3 had fully landed (about 3175), and the spin
    would end at about 4.9 s.
  </p>

  <h2>Replay three: a press at t = 1800</h2>

  <p>
    Take the teased variant and suppose the player taps skip at
    <code>t = 1800</code>, with the tease protected by <code>protect: 'once'</code>.
    At that instant reels 0 to 2 are, from the engine's point of view, still
    up: reel 2 landed at about 1497 but is bouncing until about 2097, and a
    reel only counts as landed once its bounce is done. Reels 3 and 4 are
    teasing. The press finds protected tease reels, so what it frees is
    everything except reels 3 and 4: those three are force-completed, placed on
    their frames and land in the same tick. The player sees three columns slam
    while the two teasing columns keep crawling, which is exactly the point of
    protection.
  </p>

  <p>
    Because something is still spinning, the skip stage moves to 1 and the
    once-per-round speed boost waits. A second press finds the
    <code>'once'</code> protection spent, slams reels 3 and 4, moves the stage
    to 2 and applies the boost, emitting <code>skip:boosted</code>. Every
    variant of this is on the <a href="/architecture/skipping/">skipping page</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>What TURBO and SUPER_TURBO change</h2>

  <p>
    The faster profiles do not change the script, only the numbers in it. The
    table lists each key, its value on the three presets, and where on the
    replay above it bites.
  </p>
  <div class="not-prose my-6">
    <div class="rounded-lg border border-border bg-card overflow-hidden">
      <table class="w-full text-sm">
        <thead class="bg-muted/60">
          <tr>
            {['Key', 'NORMAL', 'TURBO', 'SUPER_TURBO', 'Where it lands on this timeline'].map((h) => (
              <th class="text-left px-4 py-2 font-mono text-[11px] uppercase tracking-wider text-muted-foreground">{h}</th>
            ))}
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {[
            ['spinDelay', '100', '30', '0', 'start.delay per reel: i x value'],
            ['accelerationDuration', '300', '200', '50', 'start.accelerate; plus the 50 ms pull'],
            ['spinSpeed', '30', '50', '80', 'target of accelerate; spin-out and tease speeds derive from it'],
            ['minimumSpinTime', '500', '300', '100', 'per reel, from its own spin entry'],
            ['stopDelay', '140', '0', '0', 'stop.delay per reel: i x value'],
            ['anticipationDelay', '450', '250', '0', 'tease hold; 0 means no tease at all'],
            ['bounceDistance / bounceDuration', '56 / 600', '42 / 200', '14 / 120', 'stop.bounce'],
            ['accelerationEase', 'power2.in', 'power2.in', 'power1.in', 'start.accelerate'],
          ].map(([k, n, t, s, w]) => (
            <tr class="border-t border-border/60">
              <td class="px-4 py-2.5 font-mono text-primary text-xs">{k}</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-2.5 font-mono text-xs text-foreground/80">{n}</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-2.5 font-mono text-xs text-foreground/80">{t}</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-2.5 font-mono text-xs text-foreground/80">{s}</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-xs text-foreground/80">{w}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </div>
  <p>
    On TURBO the staircase is almost flat: the five reels enter spin at 250,
    280, 310, 340 and 370 (a 30 ms stagger, the 50 ms pull, a 200 ms
    acceleration). With the result at 1000 every reel is past its 300 ms
    minimum, so all five complete spin at 1000, and because
    <code>stopDelay</code> is 0 all five start spinning out in the same tick
    and bounce for 200 ms. To the player the columns start together, blur,
    and drop together with a short, firm bounce. TURBO still hesitates when
    asked: its 250 ms <code>anticipationDelay</code> keeps the tease alive.
  </p>

  <p>
    SUPER_TURBO goes further: no start stagger, a 50 ms acceleration, a 100 ms
    minimum, no stop stagger, a 120 ms bounce, and no tease at all unless
    <code>setAnticipation</code> is given a positive <code>duration</code>. All
    three presets have a <code>bounceDistance</code> above zero, so all three
    play the backward pull at the start. The one thing no profile fixes is the
    spin-out length: it is however many frames the strip needs to wrap five
    symbols at the speed the reel is doing, and <code>StandardMode</code> caps
    that at half a cell per frame. The full preset table is on the
    <a href="/architecture/speed-profiles/">speed profiles page</a>; the
    <a href="/guides/speed-modes/">speed modes guide</a> shows how to switch
    between them.
  </p>

  <Callout kind="gotcha">
    A reel is not "landed" when it touches the grid; it is landed when its
    bounce has finished. <code>spin:reelLanding</code> fires at the touch,
    <code>spin:reelLanded</code> after the bounce, and only the second one
    counts for skip presses, group barriers and <code>spin:complete</code>. On
    NORMAL that is a 600 ms gap per reel.
  </Callout>

  <div class="not-prose my-6 rounded-lg border border-border bg-card p-4">
    <div class="text-[11px] font-mono uppercase tracking-wider text-brand-text mb-1">Where this lives</div>
    <ul class="text-sm space-y-1 text-foreground/80">
      <li><code>packages/pixi-reels/src/config/SpeedPresets.ts</code>: the three presets</li>
      <li><code>packages/pixi-reels/src/spin/SpinController.ts</code>: <code>_startReel</code>, <code>_stopDelayFor</code>, <code>_tryBeginStopSequence</code>, <code>_markLanded</code></li>
      <li><code>packages/pixi-reels/src/spin/phases/SpinPhase.ts</code>: the minimum spin time, counted in ticker deltas</li>
      <li><code>packages/pixi-reels/src/spin/phases/StopPhase.ts</code>: the delay, spin-out, land and bounce steps</li>
      <li>Next: <a href="/architecture/skipping/">skipping</a>, <a href="/architecture/anticipation/">anticipation</a>, <a href="/architecture/events/">events</a></li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <Callout kind="remember">
    On NORMAL with a one-second result, the first reel lands at about 1.2 s
    and the last settles at about 2.6 s. Two staggers add up when the result is
    early, a tease adds about 840 ms for two reels, and nothing counts as
    landed until it has stopped bouncing.
  </Callout>
</Architecture>
